{%- comment -%}
  FAQ LIST — renders a set of FAQ metaobject entries as accessible disclosures.

  Slice 12. ONE renderer, used by both FAQ surfaces:
    - sections/faq.liquid        the FAQ page, all entries, grouped by category
    - snippets/product-faq.liquid  contextual FAQs on a product page

  A second copy of this markup would be two places an answer could render
  differently, which is the same failure the policy-terms design exists to
  prevent — so both callers render through here.

  ============================================================================
  NO JAVASCRIPT, NO APP PROXY, DELIBERATELY
  ============================================================================
  Unlike `policy-terms.liquid`, which must fetch from our own Postgres through
  the App Proxy, FAQ content lives in Shopify metaobjects and is available to
  Liquid directly. So this renders server-side with no fetch, no loading
  state, and no failure path to design — the content is either in the
  metaobject or it is not.

  `<details>`/`<summary>` is native disclosure: keyboard-operable and
  screen-reader-announced with no script, and it matches the pattern already
  shipped in `snippets/product-disclosures.liquid`.

  ============================================================================
  THE FIELD KEYS ARE A CONTRACT WITH THE ADMIN DEFINITION
  ============================================================================
  Metaobject type handle: `faq_entry`
  Fields read here:  question, answer, category, sort_key

  `docs/specs/SLICE-12-CONTENT.md` §4.2 specifies those four. If the Admin
  definition uses different keys, entries render blank rather than erroring —
  Liquid returns nil for an unknown field — so `app/app/theme/faqSurface.test.ts`
  pins these names against the spec.

  ANSWER IS RENDERED AS THE FIELD TYPE GIVES IT. A rich-text metaobject field
  returns safe HTML that Shopify itself produced; a multi-line text field
  returns a string, which Liquid escapes on output. Either is correct. What is
  NOT done is piping a plain string through anything that would interpret it
  as markup.

  ACCEPTS:
    entries        array of faq_entry metaobjects (required)
    heading        optional heading text
    heading_size   h2 | h3 (default h2)
    show_category  true to print the category above each group
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign faq_heading_size = heading_size | default: 'h2'
-%}

{%- if entries != blank and entries.size > 0 -%}
  <div class="carat-faq">
    {%- if heading != blank -%}
      <h2 class="carat-faq__heading {{ faq_heading_size }}">{{ heading | escape }}</h2>
    {%- endif -%}

    <div class="carat-faq__list">
      {%- comment -%}
        ORDERING, IN TWO PASSES — AND THE REASON IS NIL SAFETY.

        `sort_key` is OPTIONAL by spec, so most entries will not have one.
        Handing a mixed array to `sort: 'sort_key'` asks Liquid to compare a
        number against nil, which is exactly the kind of thing that behaves
        differently from one Liquid version to the next and cannot be proven
        from here without live metaobjects.

        So: entries WITH a key are sorted among themselves and printed first,
        then entries WITHOUT one follow in their natural order. No nil ever
        reaches a comparison, the result is deterministic, and the optional
        key does what staff want — it promotes an entry above the rest
        ("How long will my piece take?" before narrower questions) without
        requiring a key on every entry.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign keyed = entries | where: 'sort_key' | sort: 'sort_key' -%}
      {%- for entry in keyed -%}
        {%- render 'faq-entry', entry: entry, show_category: show_category -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
      {%- for entry in entries -%}
        {%- if entry.sort_key == blank -%}
          {%- render 'faq-entry', entry: entry, show_category: show_category -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    </div>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
